    "### Step 6: Dataset Partitioning and Class Balancing (Split & Balance)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What?**\n",
    "The definitive dataset is separated into at least two independent bodies: the Training set and the Validation/Test set. Before splitting, an ultimate check analyzes the target variable's distribution. If a critical class imbalance is detected, synthetic mathematical generators (like SMOTE) or under-sampling mechanisms can be deployed.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why?**\n",
    "A model cannot be honestly evaluated on the same data it learned from, as this creates an illusion of performance known as \"Overfitting\". Additionally, if the objective is predicting banking fraud (which is exceedingly rare), the model will statistically prefer to predict \"no fraud\" 100% of the time. Balancing the classes forces the algorithm to legitimately learn the underlying patterns of the rare minority group.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Inputs:**\n",
    "- The fully encoded, numerical dataset and the identified Target variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outputs:**\n",
    "- Isolated `X_train`, `X_test`, `y_train`, `y_test` matrices (stored in `state.data_splits`).\n",
    "- An artificially balanced training set (if resampling methods are applied).\n"
   ]
